NEW_LESSONS["building_rest_apis_with_python"] = [
    {
        "title": "Lesson 11: Middleware & CORS",
        "html": "<h2>Custom Middleware and Cross-Origin Requests</h2><p>Middleware processes requests globally before they reach route handlers. CORS is essential for web apps calling your API from different domains.</p><h3>Custom Timing Middleware:</h3><pre><code>from fastapi import FastAPI, Request\nfrom starlette.middleware.base import BaseHTTPMiddleware\nimport time\n\nclass TimingMiddleware(BaseHTTPMiddleware):\n    async def dispatch(self, request: Request, call_next):\n        start = time.perf_counter()\n        response = await call_next(request)\n        process_time = time.perf_counter() - start\n        response.headers[\"X-Process-Time\"] = str(process_time)\n        return response\n\napp = FastAPI()\napp.add_middleware(TimingMiddleware)</code></pre><h3>CORS Configuration:</h3><pre><code>from fastapi.middleware.cors import CORSMiddleware\n\norigins = [\"http://localhost:3000\", \"https://myapp.example.com\"]\n\napp.add_middleware(\n    CORSMiddleware,\n    allow_origins=origins,\n    allow_credentials=True,\n    allow_methods=[\"GET\", \"POST\", \"PUT\", \"DELETE\", \"PATCH\"],\n    allow_headers=[\"*\"],\n    expose_headers=[\"X-Request-ID\", \"X-Process-Time\"],\n    max_age=600,\n)</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Create a middleware that logs all requests to a database</li><li>Implement rate limiting middleware using token bucket</li><li>Add request ID propagation middleware for tracing</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>Middleware wraps all route handlers</li><li>CORS must be configured for browser-based clients</li><li>Middleware order matters for request processing</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 12: File Uploads & Streaming",
        "html": "<h2>Handling File Uploads and Streaming Responses</h2><p>FastAPI handles file uploads with UploadFile. Streaming responses serve large files without loading them into memory.</p><h3>File Upload:</h3><pre><code>from fastapi import FastAPI, UploadFile, File\nimport shutil\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\napp = FastAPI()\nUPLOAD_DIR = Path(\"./uploads\")\nUPLOAD_DIR.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n\n@app.post(\"/files/upload/\")\nasync def upload_file(file: UploadFile = File(...)):\n    file_path = UPLOAD_DIR / file.filename\n    with file_path.open(\"wb\") as buffer:\n        shutil.copyfileobj(file.file, buffer)\n    return {\"filename\": file.filename, \"size\": file_path.stat().st_size}</code></pre><h3>Streaming CSV:</h3><pre><code>from fastapi.responses import StreamingResponse\nimport csv, io\n\n@app.get(\"/export/users/csv/\")\nasync def export_users_csv():\n    def generate():\n        output = io.StringIO()\n        writer = csv.writer(output)\n        writer.writerow([\"ID\", \"Name\", \"Email\"])\n        for i in range(10000):\n            writer.writerow([i, f\"User {i}\", f\"user{i}@ex.com\"])\n            yield output.getvalue()\n            output.seek(0)\n            output.truncate(0)\n    return StreamingResponse(generate(), media_type=\"text/csv\",\n        headers={\"Content-Disposition\": \"attachment; filename=users.csv\"})</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Build an image upload endpoint with thumbnail generation</li><li>Stream video files with range request support</li><li>Implement multipart upload for large files</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>UploadFile streams files without loading entirely in memory</li><li>StreamingResponse is ideal for large datasets</li><li>Generator functions yield data lazily</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 13: WebSockets with FastAPI",
        "html": "<h2>Real-Time Communication with WebSockets</h2><p>WebSockets enable bidirectional, real-time communication between clients and servers.</p><h3>Connection Manager:</h3><pre><code>from fastapi import FastAPI, WebSocket, WebSocketDisconnect\nfrom typing import Set\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\nclass ConnectionManager:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.active: Set[WebSocket] = set()\n\n    async def connect(self, ws: WebSocket):\n        await ws.accept()\n        self.active.add(ws)\n\n    def disconnect(self, ws: WebSocket):\n        self.active.remove(ws)\n\n    async def broadcast(self, message: str):\n        for conn in self.active:\n            await conn.send_text(message)\n\nmanager = ConnectionManager()</code></pre><h3>WebSocket Endpoint:</h3><pre><code>@app.websocket(\"/ws/{client_id}\")\nasync def websocket_endpoint(ws: WebSocket, client_id: str):\n    await manager.connect(ws)\n    try:\n        await manager.broadcast(f\"Client {client_id} joined\")\n        while True:\n            data = await ws.receive_text()\n            await manager.broadcast(f\"{client_id}: {data}\")\n    except WebSocketDisconnect:\n        manager.disconnect(ws)\n        await manager.broadcast(f\"{client_id} left\")</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Build a real-time chat application with rooms</li><li>Implement a live notification system</li><li>Add WebSocket authentication middleware</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>WebSockets provide full-duplex communication</li><li>Connection manager tracks active clients</li><li>WebSocketDisconnect signals client departure</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 14: Rate Limiting & Security Headers",
        "html": "<h2>API Protection and Security</h2><p>Rate limiting prevents abuse. Security headers protect against common web vulnerabilities.</p><h3>Rate Limiting:</h3><pre><code>from slowapi import Limiter, _rate_limit_exceeded_handler\nfrom slowapi.util import get_remote_address\nfrom slowapi.errors import RateLimitExceeded\n\nlimiter = Limiter(key_func=get_remote_address)\napp = FastAPI()\napp.state.limiter = limiter\napp.add_exception_handler(RateLimitExceeded, _rate_limit_exceeded_handler)\n\n@app.get(\"/api/public/\")\n@limiter.limit(\"10/minute\")\nasync def public_endpoint():\n    return {\"message\": \"rate limited\"}</code></pre><h3>Security Headers:</h3><pre><code>class SecurityHeadersMiddleware(BaseHTTPMiddleware):\n    async def dispatch(self, request, call_next):\n        response = await call_next(request)\n        response.headers[\"X-Content-Type-Options\"] = \"nosniff\"\n        response.headers[\"X-Frame-Options\"] = \"DENY\"\n        response.headers[\"Strict-Transport-Security\"] = \"max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains\"\n        response.headers[\"Content-Security-Policy\"] = \"default-src 'self'\"\n        response.headers[\"Permissions-Policy\"] = \"camera=(), microphone=()\"\n        return response</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Set up tiered rate limiting (free/paid tiers)</li><li>Implement API key authentication with rate limiting per key</li><li>Scan headers for security vulnerabilities</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>Rate limiting protects against DDoS and abuse</li><li>Security headers mitigate XSS, clickjacking, and MIME sniffing</li><li>HTTPS + security headers = defense in depth</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 15: API Documentation & SDK Generation",
        "html": "<h2>Auto-Generated Clients and Documentation</h2><p>FastAPI generates OpenAPI docs automatically. Use them to generate type-safe SDKs.</p><h3>Custom OpenAPI:</h3><pre><code>app = FastAPI(\n    title=\"Enterprise API\",\n    description=\"Complete API with auth and admin features\",\n    version=\"2.0.0\",\n    contact={\"name\": \"Support\", \"email\": \"support@example.com\"},\n    license_info={\"name\": \"MIT\", \"url\": \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\"},\n)</code></pre><h3>SDK Generation:</h3><pre><code># pip install openapi-python-client\n# openapi-python-client generate --url http://localhost:8000/openapi.json\n\n# Generated SDK usage:\nfrom my_api_client import Client\nfrom my_api_client.api.users import get_users, create_user\nfrom my_api_client.models import UserCreate\n\nclient = Client(base_url=\"http://localhost:8000\")\nusers = get_users.sync(client=client)\nnew_user = create_user.sync(client=client,\n    body=UserCreate(email=\"alice@ex.com\", name=\"Alice\", password=\"pass123\"))</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Customize OpenAPI tags with detailed descriptions</li><li>Generate Python SDK and use it in tests</li><li>Add examples to your Pydantic models for docs</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>OpenAPI provides machine-readable API specs</li><li>Auto-generated SDKs are type-safe</li><li>Good documentation reduces integration time</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 16: GraphQL with Strawberry",
        "html": "<h2>GraphQL API with Python</h2><p>GraphQL lets clients request exactly the data they need. Strawberry is a modern GraphQL library.</p><h3>Schema Definition:</h3><pre><code>import strawberry\nfrom typing import List, Optional\n\n@strawberry.type\nclass User:\n    id: strawberry.ID\n    name: str\n    email: str\n    posts: List[\"Post\"]\n\n@strawberry.type\nclass Post:\n    id: strawberry.ID\n    title: str\n    content: str\n    author: User\n\n@strawberry.type\nclass Query:\n    @strawberry.field\n    def users(self) -> List[User]:\n        return get_all_users()\n\n    @strawberry.field\n    def user(self, id: strawberry.ID) -> Optional[User]:\n        return get_user_by_id(id)</code></pre><h3>FastAPI Integration:</h3><pre><code>from strawberry.fastapi import GraphQLRouter\n\nschema = strawberry.Schema(query=Query)\ngraphql_app = GraphQLRouter(schema)\napp.include_router(graphql_app, prefix=\"/graphql\")</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Design a GraphQL schema for an e-commerce app</li><li>Add mutations for create/update/delete operations</li><li>Implement DataLoader for N+1 prevention</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>GraphQL returns only requested fields</li><li>Strawberry generates type-safe resolvers</li><li>DataLoader solves N+1 query problems</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 17: Celery Task Queue",
        "html": "<h2>Background Task Processing with Celery</h2><p>Celery is the most popular distributed task queue for Python. Handles email, image processing, and report generation.</p><h3>Celery Setup:</h3><pre><code>from celery import Celery\n\ncelery_app = Celery(\n    \"tasks\",\n    broker=\"redis://localhost:6379/0\",\n    backend=\"redis://localhost:6379/0\"\n)\n\ncelery_app.conf.update(\n    task_serializer=\"json\",\n    result_serializer=\"json\",\n    timezone=\"UTC\",\n    task_track_started=True,\n    task_acks_late=True,\n)\n\n@celery_app.task(bind=True, max_retries=3, default_retry_delay=60)\ndef send_email_task(self, to_email, subject, body):\n    try:\n        # send email\n        return {\"status\": \"sent\", \"to\": to_email}\n    except Exception as exc:\n        raise self.retry(exc=exc)</code></pre><h3>FastAPI Integration:</h3><pre><code>@app.post(\"/send-email/\")\nasync def send_email(request: EmailRequest):\n    task = send_email_task.delay(request.to, request.subject, request.body)\n    return {\"message\": \"Queued\", \"task_id\": task.id}\n\n@app.get(\"/tasks/{task_id}/\")\nasync def get_task_status(task_id: str):\n    task = send_email_task.AsyncResult(task_id)\n    if task.failed():\n        return {\"status\": \"failed\", \"error\": str(task.info)}\n    return {\"status\": task.state}</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Create a task that generates PDF reports</li><li>Implement task chaining for multi-step workflows</li><li>Monitor Celery workers with Flower dashboard</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>Tasks run asynchronously outside the request cycle</li><li>Retries with backoff handle transient failures</li><li>Task IDs allow status polling from clients</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 18: API Caching with Redis",
        "html": "<h2>Redis Caching Strategies for APIs</h2><p>Caching reduces database load and improves response times significantly.</p><h3>Cache Decorator:</h3><pre><code>import redis, json, hashlib\nfrom functools import wraps\n\nredis_client = redis.Redis(host=\"localhost\", port=6379, db=0, decode_responses=True)\n\ndef cache(ttl_seconds=300):\n    def decorator(func):\n        @wraps(func)\n        async def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n            key_parts = [func.__name__] + [str(a) for a in args]\n            cache_key = hashlib.md5(\"|\".join(key_parts).encode()).hexdigest()\n            cached = redis_client.get(cache_key)\n            if cached:\n                return json.loads(cached)\n            result = await func(*args, **kwargs)\n            redis_client.setex(cache_key, ttl_seconds, json.dumps(result))\n            return result\n        return wrapper\n    return decorator\n\n@app.get(\"/api/popular/\")\n@cache(ttl_seconds=60)\nasync def get_popular():\n    return db.query(User).order_by(User.followers.desc()).limit(10).all()</code></pre><h3>Cache Invalidation:</h3><pre><code>class CacheManager:\n    def invalidate_pattern(self, pattern: str):\n        cursor = 0\n        while True:\n            cursor, keys = self.redis.scan(cursor, match=pattern, count=100)\n            if keys:\n                self.redis.delete(*keys)\n            if cursor == 0:\n                break\n\n    async def after_user_update(self, user_id):\n        self.invalidate_pattern(f\"get_user*{user_id}*\")\n        self.invalidate_pattern(\"get_popular*\")</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Implement write-through vs write-behind caching</li><li>Add distributed caching with Redis Cluster</li><li>Monitor cache hit rate and optimize TTLs</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>Caching improves API response times by 10-100x</li><li>Cache invalidation is the hardest part of caching</li><li>Redis supports complex data structures beyond simple key-value</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 19: API Versioning Strategies",
        "html": "<h2>Smooth API Versioning</h2><p>API versioning lets you evolve your API without breaking existing clients.</p><h3>URL Path Versioning:</h3><pre><code>from fastapi import APIRouter\n\nv1 = APIRouter(prefix=\"/api/v1\", tags=[\"v1\"])\nv2 = APIRouter(prefix=\"/api/v2\", tags=[\"v2\"])\n\n@v1.get(\"/users/\")\nasync def get_users_v1():\n    return [{\"id\": 1, \"name\": \"Alice\", \"email\": \"alice@ex.com\"}]\n\n@v2.get(\"/users/\")\nasync def get_users_v2(page: int = 1, per_page: int = 20):\n    users = get_users_paginated(page, per_page)\n    return {\"data\": users, \"pagination\": {\"page\": page, \"per_page\": per_page,\n        \"total\": count_users()}}\n\napp.include_router(v1)\napp.include_router(v2)</code></pre><h3>Header Versioning:</h3><pre><code>@app.get(\"/api/users/\")\nasync def get_users(request: Request):\n    version = request.headers.get(\"Accept-Version\", \"2025-01-01\")\n    handlers = {\"2023-01-01\": handle_v1, \"2025-01-01\": handle_v2}\n    if version not in handlers:\n        raise HTTPException(400, f\"Unsupported version: {version}\")\n    return await handlers[version](request)</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Design versioned API for a social media platform</li><li>Implement sunset headers for deprecated versions</li><li>Create migration guides for breaking changes</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>URL versioning is simplest but leads to URL duplication</li><li>Header versioning keeps URLs clean</li><li>Always document deprecated features with sunset dates</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 20: Production Monitoring & Health Checks",
        "html": "<h2>API Health Checks and Structured Logging</h2><p>Production APIs need health checks, monitoring, and structured logging for reliability.</p><h3>Health Check:</h3><pre><code>from pydantic import BaseModel\nimport psutil, time\nfrom datetime import datetime\n\nclass HealthStatus(BaseModel):\n    status: str\n    version: str\n    timestamp: str\n    database: str\n    redis: str\n    memory_usage: float\n    cpu_percent: float\n\napp = FastAPI()\nstart_time = time.time()\n\n@app.get(\"/health/\", response_model=HealthStatus)\nasync def health():\n    db_status = \"healthy\" if check_db() else \"unhealthy\"\n    redis_status = \"healthy\" if check_redis() else \"unhealthy\"\n    return HealthStatus(\n        status=\"ok\" if db_status == \"healthy\" else \"degraded\",\n        version=\"2.0.0\",\n        timestamp=datetime.utcnow().isoformat(),\n        uptime_seconds=time.time() - start_time,\n        database=db_status,\n        redis=redis_status,\n        memory_usage=psutil.virtual_memory().percent,\n        cpu_percent=psutil.cpu_percent(interval=1),\n    )</code></pre><h3>Structured Logging:</h3><pre><code>from loguru import logger\nimport sys\n\nlogger.configure(handlers=[\n    {\"sink\": sys.stdout, \"serialize\": True},\n    {\"sink\": \"logs/api.log\", \"rotation\": \"500 MB\", \"retention\": \"30 days\", \"serialize\": True},\n])\n\n@app.middleware(\"http\")\nasync def log_requests(request, call_next):\n    start = time.time()\n    response = await call_next(request)\n    logger.info(\"API Request\", extra={\n        \"method\": request.method, \"path\": request.url.path,\n        \"status\": response.status_code,\n        \"duration_ms\": round((time.time() - start) * 1000, 2),\n        \"client_ip\": request.client.host,\n    })\n    return response</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Set up Grafana dashboards for API metrics</li><li>Implement alerting for error rate spikes</li><li>Add structured logging with correlation IDs</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>Health checks enable automated recovery</li><li>Structured JSON logs are machine-parseable</li><li>Monitoring metrics guide capacity planning</li></ul>"
    }
]

NEW_LESSONS["data_science_with_python"] = [
    {
        "title": "Lesson 8: Advanced NumPy Operations",
        "html": "<h2>Broadcasting and Vectorization</h2><p>NumPy broadcasting allows arithmetic between arrays of different shapes. Vectorized operations are much faster than Python loops.</p><h3>Broadcasting Rules:</h3><pre><code>import numpy as np\n\na = np.array([1, 2, 3])\nb = 10\nprint(a + b)  # [11, 12, 13]\n\nA = np.array([[1], [2], [3]])\nB = np.array([[10, 20, 30]])\nprint(A + B)  # (3,1) + (1,3) -> (3,3)</code></pre><h3>Performance Comparison:</h3><pre><code>import time\n\nn = 10_000_000\n\nstart = time.time()\nresult = [i**2 for i in range(n)]\nprint(f\"Python loop: {time.time() - start:.2f}s\")\n\nstart = time.time()\narr = np.arange(n)\nresult = arr**2\nprint(f\"NumPy vectorized: {time.time() - start:.2f}s\")\n# NumPy is typically 50-100x faster!</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Implement a matrix multiplication benchmark comparing Python vs NumPy</li><li>Use broadcasting to normalize a dataset without loops</li><li>Create a custom ufunc using numpy.frompyfunc</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>Broadcasting avoids unnecessary memory allocation</li><li>Vectorized operations use optimized C/Fortran code</li><li>Broadcasting rules: right-align dimensions, missing dims become 1</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 9: Time Series Analysis",
        "html": "<h2>Working with Time Series Data</h2><p>Time series data has trends, seasonality, and autocorrelation. Pandas provides powerful time-based analysis tools.</p><h3>Date Operations:</h3><pre><code>import pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\n\ndates = pd.date_range(\"2023-01-01\", periods=365, freq=\"D\")\nts = pd.Series(np.random.randn(365), index=dates)\n\n# Resampling\nweekly = ts.resample(\"W\").mean()\nmonthly = ts.resample(\"M\").agg([\"mean\", \"std\", \"min\", \"max\"])\n\n# Rolling windows\nrolling_mean = ts.rolling(window=7).mean()\nrolling_std = ts.rolling(window=7).std()\n\n# Shifts and diffs\nts_lag = ts.shift(1)      # Previous value\nts_diff = ts.diff()       # Day-over-day change\nts_pct = ts.pct_change()  # Percentage change</code></pre><h3>Stock Price Analysis:</h3><pre><code>np.random.seed(42)\nreturns = np.random.normal(0.0001, 0.02, 252)\nprice = 100 * np.exp(np.cumsum(returns))\n\nstock = pd.DataFrame({\"price\": price, \"returns\": returns},\n    index=pd.date_range(\"2023-01-01\", periods=252, freq=\"B\"))\n\nstock[\"sma_20\"] = stock[\"price\"].rolling(20).mean()\nstock[\"sma_50\"] = stock[\"price\"].rolling(50).mean()\nstock[\"volatility\"] = stock[\"returns\"].rolling(20).std() * np.sqrt(252)\nstock[\"signal\"] = np.where(stock[\"sma_20\"] > stock[\"sma_50\"], 1, -1)</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Detect seasonality in a monthly sales dataset</li><li>Implement a moving average crossover trading strategy</li><li>Forecast time series using ARIMA or Prophet</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>Resampling changes the frequency of time series data</li><li>Rolling windows smooth noise and reveal trends</li><li>Stationarity is required for many forecasting models</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 10: Feature Engineering",
        "html": "<h2>Creating Better Features for ML Models</h2><p>Feature engineering transforms raw data into features that better represent the underlying problem.</p><h3>Numeric Transformations:</h3><pre><code>from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler, MinMaxScaler, PolynomialFeatures\n\nnp.random.seed(42)\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"age\": np.random.randint(18, 80, 1000),\n    \"income\": np.random.exponential(50000, 1000),\n    \"transactions\": np.random.poisson(10, 1000),\n})\n\n# Log transform for skewed data\ndf[\"income_log\"] = np.log1p(df[\"income\"])\n\n# Polynomial features\npoly = PolynomialFeatures(degree=2, include_bias=False)\npoly_features = poly.fit_transform(df[[\"age\", \"income_log\"]])\npoly_names = poly.get_feature_names_out([\"age\", \"income_log\"])\nprint(\"Polynomial features:\", poly_names)</code></pre><h3>Categorical Encoding:</h3><pre><code># One-hot encoding\ndf = pd.get_dummies(df, columns=[\"city\", \"education\"], drop_first=True)\n\n# Target encoding\nfrom category_encoders import TargetEncoder\nencoder = TargetEncoder(cols=[\"city\"])\ndf[\"city_encoded\"] = encoder.fit_transform(df[\"city\"], df[\"income\"])</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Create interaction features between age and income</li><li>Apply PCA for dimensionality reduction</li><li>Build a feature pipeline with ColumnTransformer</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>Log transforms handle skewed distributions</li><li>Polynomial features capture non-linear relationships</li><li>Good features matter more than good models</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 11: Model Evaluation & Cross-Validation",
        "html": "<h2>Proper Model Evaluation Techniques</h2><p>Cross-validation prevents overfitting and gives realistic performance estimates.</p><h3>Cross-Validation:</h3><pre><code>from sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score, KFold\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error\n\nX = np.random.randn(1000, 10)\ny = X[:, 0] * 2 + X[:, 1] * (-1) + np.random.randn(1000) * 0.1\n\nmodel = RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=100, random_state=42)\n\ncv_scores = cross_val_score(model, X, y, cv=5, scoring=\"neg_mean_squared_error\")\nrmse_scores = np.sqrt(-cv_scores)\nprint(f\"CV RMSE: {rmse_scores.mean():.3f} (+/- {rmse_scores.std() * 2:.3f})\")</code></pre><h3>Learning Curves:</h3><pre><code>from sklearn.model_selection import learning_curve\n\ntrain_sizes, train_scores, test_scores = learning_curve(\n    model, X, y, cv=5, train_sizes=np.linspace(0.1, 1.0, 10),\n    scoring=\"neg_mean_squared_error\", n_jobs=-1\n)\n\ntrain_rmse = np.sqrt(-train_scores.mean(axis=1))\ntest_rmse = np.sqrt(-test_scores.mean(axis=1))</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Compare different cross-validation strategies (KFold, Stratified, Group)</li><li>Use learning curves to diagnose bias vs variance</li><li>Implement nested cross-validation for model selection</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>Cross-validation uses all data for both training and validation</li><li>Learning curves show bias/variance tradeoff</li><li>Stratified CV preserves class distribution</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 12: Ensemble Methods - Random Forest & XGBoost",
        "html": "<h2>Ensemble Methods Deep Dive</h2><p>Ensemble methods combine multiple models for better predictions. Random Forest and XGBoost are the most popular.</p><h3>Random Forest Tuning:</h3><pre><code>from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import RandomizedSearchCV\n\nrf = RandomForestRegressor(random_state=42)\nrf_params = {\n    \"n_estimators\": [100, 200, 500],\n    \"max_depth\": [5, 10, 20, None],\n    \"min_samples_split\": [2, 5, 10],\n    \"max_features\": [\"sqrt\", \"log2\", None],\n}\n\nrf_search = RandomizedSearchCV(rf, rf_params, n_iter=20, cv=3,\n    scoring=\"neg_mean_squared_error\", n_jobs=-1, random_state=42)\nrf_search.fit(X, y)\nprint(f\"Best params: {rf_search.best_params_}\")</code></pre><h3>XGBoost:</h3><pre><code>import xgboost as xgb\n\nparams = {\n    \"objective\": \"reg:squarederror\",\n    \"learning_rate\": 0.1,\n    \"max_depth\": 6,\n    \"subsample\": 0.8,\n    \"colsample_bytree\": 0.8,\n    \"reg_alpha\": 1.0,\n    \"reg_lambda\": 2.0,\n}\n\ndtrain = xgb.DMatrix(X_train, label=y_train)\ndtest = xgb.DMatrix(X_test, label=y_test)\nmodel = xgb.train(params, dtrain, num_boost_round=100,\n    evals=[(dtest, \"test\")], early_stopping_rounds=10, verbose_eval=False)\n\nimportance = model.get_score(importance_type=\"gain\")\nprint(\"Top features:\", sorted(importance.items(), key=lambda x: x[1], reverse=True)[:5])</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Compare RF vs XGBoost performance on a dataset</li><li>Tune hyperparameters with Bayesian optimization</li><li>Implement stacking with multiple base models</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>Random Forest reduces overfitting through bagging</li><li>XGBoost uses gradient boosting for sequential improvement</li><li>Feature importance helps interpret ensemble models</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 13: Deep Learning with PyTorch",
        "html": "<h2>Neural Network Fundamentals</h2><p>PyTorch provides flexible tensor operations and automatic differentiation for building neural networks.</p><h3>Simple Neural Network:</h3><pre><code>import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport torch.optim as optim\n\nX = torch.randn(1000, 10)\ny = (X[:, 0] * 2 + X[:, 1] * (-1) > 0).float().unsqueeze(1)\n\nclass SimpleNN(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.net = nn.Sequential(\n            nn.Linear(10, 32), nn.ReLU(), nn.Dropout(0.2),\n            nn.Linear(32, 16), nn.ReLU(),\n            nn.Linear(16, 1), nn.Sigmoid()\n        )\n    def forward(self, x):\n        return self.net(x)\n\nmodel = SimpleNN()\ncriterion = nn.BCELoss()\noptimizer = optim.Adam(model.parameters(), lr=0.001)</code></pre><h3>Training Loop:</h3><pre><code>dataset = TensorDataset(X, y)\nloader = DataLoader(dataset, batch_size=32, shuffle=True)\n\nfor epoch in range(100):\n    model.train()\n    total_loss = 0\n    for batch_X, batch_y in loader:\n        optimizer.zero_grad()\n        loss = criterion(model(batch_X), batch_y)\n        loss.backward()\n        optimizer.step()\n        total_loss += loss.item()\n    if (epoch+1) % 10 == 0:\n        print(f\"Epoch {epoch+1}, Loss: {total_loss/len(loader):.4f}\")</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Build a CNN for image classification on CIFAR-10</li><li>Implement an LSTM for time series forecasting</li><li>Use transfer learning with a pre-trained model</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>Automatic differentiation computes gradients</li><li>GPU acceleration speeds up training 10-100x</li><li>DataLoaders handle batching and shuffling</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 14: Natural Language Processing",
        "html": "<h2>Text Processing and Classification</h2><p>NLP enables computers to understand human language. Build a text classification pipeline.</p><h3>Text Preprocessing:</h3><pre><code>import nltk\nfrom nltk.stem import WordNetLemmatizer\nfrom nltk.corpus import stopwords\nimport re\n\nnltk.download(\"stopwords\")\nnltk.download(\"wordnet\")\n\nlemmatizer = WordNetLemmatizer()\nstop_words = set(stopwords.words(\"english\"))\n\ndef preprocess(text: str) -> str:\n    text = text.lower()\n    text = re.sub(r\"[^a-zA-Z\\s]\", \"\", text)\n    words = [lemmatizer.lemmatize(w) for w in text.split()\n             if w not in stop_words and len(w) > 2]\n    return \" \".join(words)</code></pre><h3>TF-IDF Classification:</h3><pre><code>from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer\nfrom sklearn.naive_bayes import MultinomialNB\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\n\ncorpus = [\"I love this product\", \"Amazing quality\",\n          \"Terrible experience\", \"Worst purchase ever\"]\nlabels = [1, 1, 0, 0]\n\npipeline = Pipeline([\n    (\"tfidf\", TfidfVectorizer(preprocessor=preprocess,\n        max_features=1000, ngram_range=(1, 2), stop_words=\"english\")),\n    (\"classifier\", MultinomialNB(alpha=0.1))\n])\n\npipeline.fit(corpus, labels)\nprint(pipeline.predict([\"Excellent product\"]))  # [1]</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Build a sentiment analyzer for movie reviews</li><li>Implement named entity recognition with spaCy</li><li>Create a text summarization system</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>TF-IDF weights word importance based on frequency and rarity</li><li>N-grams capture phrase-level information</li><li>Stop words removal reduces noise in text data</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 15: Dask for Big Data",
        "html": "<h2>Parallel Computing with Dask</h2><p>Dask scales Python to datasets larger than memory using parallel computing with a familiar Pandas-like API.</p><h3>Dask DataFrames:</h3><pre><code>import dask.dataframe as dd\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Create from pandas\npdf = pd.DataFrame({\"id\": range(10000), \"group\": [\"A\",\"B\",\"C\"]*3334,\n    \"value\": range(10000)})\nddf = dd.from_pandas(pdf, npartitions=4)\n\n# Lazy evaluation - builds a task graph\nresult = ddf.groupby(\"group\")[\"value\"].agg([\"mean\", \"std\", \"count\"])\n\n# Compute triggers execution\nprint(result.compute())</code></pre><h3>Dask Delayed:</h3><pre><code>from dask import delayed\nimport time\n\n@delayed\ndef process_chunk(chunk_id):\n    time.sleep(1)\n    return {\"chunk\": chunk_id, \"result\": chunk_id**2}\n\n@delayed\ndef aggregate(results):\n    return {\"total\": sum(r[\"result\"] for r in results), \"count\": len(results)}\n\nchunks = [process_chunk(i) for i in range(10)]\nfinal_result = aggregate(chunks).compute()\nprint(f\"Aggregated: {final_result}\")</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Process a large CSV with Dask and compare to Pandas</li><li>Build a distributed computation with Dask Delayed</li><li>Use Dask ML for distributed model training</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>Dask partitions data for parallel processing</li><li>Lazy evaluation optimizes computation</li><li>Dask scales from single machine to clusters</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 16: ETL Pipelines with Airflow",
        "html": "<h2>Orchestrating Data Pipelines</h2><p>Apache Airflow orchestrates complex data pipelines with scheduling, monitoring, and error handling.</p><h3>DAG Definition:</h3><pre><code>from airflow import DAG\nfrom airflow.operators.python import PythonOperator\nfrom datetime import datetime, timedelta\nimport pandas as pd\n\ndefault_args = {\n    \"owner\": \"data_team\",\n    \"retries\": 2,\n    \"retry_delay\": timedelta(minutes=5),\n}\n\nwith DAG(\n    \"daily_pipeline\",\n    default_args=default_args,\n    schedule_interval=\"0 3 * * *\",  # Daily at 3 AM\n    start_date=datetime(2024, 1, 1),\n    catchup=False,\n) as dag:\n\n    def extract():\n        data = pd.read_sql(\"SELECT * FROM source\", conn)\n        data.to_parquet(\"/tmp/raw.parquet\")\n\n    def transform():\n        df = pd.read_parquet(\"/tmp/raw.parquet\")\n        df = df.dropna()\n        df[\"processed_at\"] = datetime.now()\n        df.to_parquet(\"/tmp/clean.parquet\")\n\n    def load():\n        df = pd.read_parquet(\"/tmp/clean.parquet\")\n        df.to_sql(\"target\", conn, if_exists=\"append\", index=False)\n\n    extract >> transform >> load</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Create a DAG with branching for conditional execution</li><li>Add SLA monitoring and email alerts on failure</li><li>Build a data quality check task between transform and load</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>DAGs define task dependencies and execution order</li><li>Airflow handles retries and failure alerts</li><li>XCom enables data passing between tasks</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 17: Model Deployment with MLflow",
        "html": "<h2>Serving ML Models in Production</h2><p>MLflow tracks experiments, packages models, and deploys them to production.</p><h3>MLflow Tracking:</h3><pre><code>import mlflow\nimport mlflow.sklearn\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import GradientBoostingRegressor\n\nmlflow.set_tracking_uri(\"http://localhost:5000\")\nmlflow.set_experiment(\"model_experiment\")\n\nwith mlflow.start_run():\n    params = {\"n_estimators\": 100, \"max_depth\": 5}\n    mlflow.log_params(params)\n\n    model = GradientBoostingRegressor(**params)\n    model.fit(X_train, y_train)\n\n    rmse = np.sqrt(mean_squared_error(y_test, model.predict(X_test)))\n    mlflow.log_metric(\"rmse\", rmse)\n    mlflow.sklearn.log_model(model, \"model\")</code></pre><h3>Model Serving:</h3><pre><code># Serve with MLflow:\n# mlflow models serve -m runs:/<run_id>/model --port 5002\n\n# Or deploy as a FastAPI endpoint:\nloaded = mlflow.sklearn.load_model(\"runs:/<run_id>/model\")\n\n@app.post(\"/predict\")\ndef predict(features: List[float]):\n    pred = loaded.predict([features])\n    return {\"prediction\": pred[0]}</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Track an experiment with hyperparameter tuning</li><li>Register the best model in MLflow Model Registry</li><li>Deploy a model as a REST API with Docker</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>MLflow tracks parameters, metrics, and artifacts</li><li>Model Registry manages model versions</li><li>MLflow can serve models as REST APIs</li></ul>"
    }
]

NEW_LESSONS["data_structures_and_algorithms_in_rust"] = [
    {
        "title": "Lesson 11: Red-Black Trees",
        "html": "<h2>Self-Balancing BST</h2><p>Red-Black trees guarantee O(log n) operations through color properties and rotations. Used in Rust's BTreeMap.</p><h3>Node Structure:</h3><pre><code>#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]\nenum Color { Red, Black }\n\nstruct RBNode&lt;T: Ord&gt; {\n    value: T,\n    color: Color,\n    left: Option&lt;Box&lt;RBNode&lt;T&gt;&gt;&gt;,\n    right: Option&lt;Box&lt;RBNode&lt;T&gt;&gt;&gt;,\n}</code></pre><h3>Insert with Fixup:</h3><pre><code>impl&lt;T: Ord&gt; RBNode&lt;T&gt; {\n    fn insert(&amp;mut self, value: T) {\n        if value &lt; self.value {\n            if let Some(ref mut left) = self.left { left.insert(value); }\n            else { self.left = Some(Box::new(RBNode::new(value))); }\n        } else if value &gt; self.value {\n            if let Some(ref mut right) = self.right { right.insert(value); }\n            else { self.right = Some(Box::new(RBNode::new(value))); }\n        }\n        self.fixup();\n    }\n\n    fn fixup(&amp;mut self) {\n        if Self::is_red(&amp;self.right) &amp;&amp; !Self::is_red(&amp;self.left) {\n            self.rotate_left();\n        }\n        if Self::is_red(&amp;self.left) &amp;&amp; Self::is_red(&amp;self.left.as_ref().unwrap().left) {\n            self.rotate_right();\n        }\n        if Self::is_red(&amp;self.left) &amp;&amp; Self::is_red(&amp;self.right) {\n            self.flip_colors();\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Implement deletion for a Red-Black tree</li><li>Compare performance with Rust's BTreeMap</li><li>Add visualization of tree rotations</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>Red-Black trees maintain balance through color rules</li><li>Rotations preserve BST property while rebalancing</li><li>Left-leaning RB trees simplify implementation</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 12: Graph Shortest Path - Dijkstra & A*",
        "html": "<h2>Pathfinding Algorithms</h2><p>Shortest path algorithms are essential for GPS navigation, network routing, and game AI.</p><h3>Dijkstra's Algorithm:</h3><pre><code>use std::collections::{HashMap, BinaryHeap};\n\n#[derive(Eq, PartialEq)]\nstruct State { cost: u32, node: usize }\n\nimpl Ord for State {\n    fn cmp(&amp;self, other: &amp;Self) -> std::cmp::Ordering {\n        other.cost.cmp(&amp;self.cost)  // Min-heap\n    }\n}\n\nfn dijkstra(adj: &amp;[Vec&lt;(usize, u32)&gt;], start: usize, end: usize) -&gt; Option&lt;u32&gt; {\n    let n = adj.len();\n    let mut dist = vec![u32::MAX; n];\n    let mut heap = BinaryHeap::new();\n\n    dist[start] = 0;\n    heap.push(State { cost: 0, node: start });\n\n    while let Some(State { cost, node }) = heap.pop() {\n        if node == end { return Some(cost); }\n        if cost &gt; dist[node] { continue; }\n        for &amp;(to, weight) in &amp;adj[node] {\n            let next = cost + weight;\n            if next &lt; dist[to] {\n                dist[to] = next;\n                heap.push(State { cost: next, node: to });\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    None\n}</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Implement A* with a heuristic function</li><li>Find the shortest path in a 2D grid with obstacles</li><li>Use Dijkstra for network routing optimization</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>Dijkstra finds shortest paths in weighted graphs</li><li>BinaryHeap provides efficient min-extraction</li><li>A* with a good heuristic is faster than Dijkstra</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 13: Disjoint Set Union (Union-Find)",
        "html": "<h2>DSU with Path Compression</h2><p>DSU tracks partitions of elements. Near O(1) operations with path compression and union by rank.</p><h3>Implementation:</h3><pre><code>struct DSU {\n    parent: Vec&lt;usize&gt;,\n    rank: Vec&lt;usize&gt;,\n    size: Vec&lt;usize&gt;,\n}\n\nimpl DSU {\n    fn new(n: usize) -> Self {\n        DSU { parent: (0..n).collect(), rank: vec![0; n], size: vec![1; n] }\n    }\n\n    fn find(&amp;mut self, x: usize) -> usize {\n        if self.parent[x] != x {\n            self.parent[x] = self.find(self.parent[x]);\n        }\n        self.parent[x]\n    }\n\n    fn union(&amp;mut self, x: usize, y: usize) -> bool {\n        let rx = self.find(x);\n        let ry = self.find(y);\n        if rx == ry { return false; }\n        match self.rank[rx].cmp(&amp;self.rank[ry]) {\n            Less =&gt; { self.parent[rx] = ry; self.size[ry] += self.size[rx]; }\n            Greater =&gt; { self.parent[ry] = rx; self.size[rx] += self.size[ry]; }\n            Equal =&gt; { self.parent[ry] = rx; self.rank[rx] += 1; self.size[rx] += self.size[ry]; }\n        }\n        true\n    }\n}</code></pre><h3>Kruskal's MST:</h3><pre><code>fn kruskal(n: usize, edges: &amp;mut [(usize, usize, u32)]) -> u32 {\n    edges.sort_by_key(|&amp;(_, _, w)| w);\n    let mut dsu = DSU::new(n);\n    edges.iter().filter(|&amp;&amp;(u, v, _)| dsu.union(u, v)).map(|&amp;(_, _, w)| w).sum()\n}</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Solve the number of connected components problem</li><li>Implement Kruskal's MST and test on random graphs</li><li>Use DSU to detect cycles in a graph</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>Path compression flattens the tree structure</li><li>Union by rank keeps trees shallow</li><li>Amortized time is O(alpha(n)) ~ constant</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 14: Segment Trees",
        "html": "<h2>Range Query Data Structure</h2><p>Segment trees answer range queries (sum, min, max) and update values in O(log n) time.</p><h3>Generic Implementation:</h3><pre><code>struct SegTree&lt;T, F&gt; {\n    n: usize,\n    data: Vec&lt;T&gt;,\n    identity: T,\n    combine: F,\n}\n\nimpl&lt;T: Clone, F: Fn(&amp;T, &amp;T) -> T&gt; SegTree&lt;T, F&gt; {\n    fn new(arr: &amp;[T], identity: T, combine: F) -> Self {\n        let n = arr.len();\n        let mut data = vec![identity.clone(); 2 * n];\n        for (i, val) in arr.iter().enumerate() { data[n + i] = val.clone(); }\n        for i in (1..n).rev() { data[i] = combine(&amp;data[2*i], &amp;data[2*i+1]); }\n        SegTree { n, data, identity, combine }\n    }\n\n    fn query(&amp;self, mut l: usize, mut r: usize) -> T {\n        l += self.n; r += self.n;\n        let mut left = self.identity.clone();\n        let mut right = self.identity.clone();\n        while l &lt; r {\n            if l &amp; 1 == 1 { left = (self.combine)(&amp;left, &amp;self.data[l]); l += 1; }\n            if r &amp; 1 == 1 { r -= 1; right = (self.combine)(&amp;self.data[r], &amp;right); }\n            l /= 2; r /= 2;\n        }\n        (self.combine)(&amp;left, &amp;right)\n    }\n\n    fn update(&amp;mut self, mut idx: usize, value: T) {\n        idx += self.n;\n        self.data[idx] = value;\n        idx /= 2;\n        while idx &gt; 0 {\n            self.data[idx] = (self.combine)(&amp;self.data[2*idx], &amp;self.data[2*idx+1]);\n            idx /= 2;\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Implement range minimum query with updates</li><li>Add lazy propagation for range updates</li><li>Use segment tree to solve the range sum problem</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>Segment trees handle point updates and range queries</li><li>The generic design works for any associative operation</li><li>Memory is 2 * next_power_of_two(n)</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 15: String Algorithms - KMP Pattern Matching",
        "html": "<h2>Efficient Substring Search</h2><p>KMP finds patterns in O(n+m) time using the prefix function (LPS array).</p><h3>KMP Implementation:</h3><pre><code>fn build_lps(pattern: &amp;[u8]) -> Vec&lt;usize&gt; {\n    let m = pattern.len();\n    let mut lps = vec![0; m];\n    let mut len = 0;\n    let mut i = 1;\n    while i &lt; m {\n        if pattern[i] == pattern[len] {\n            len += 1; lps[i] = len; i += 1;\n        } else if len &gt; 0 {\n            len = lps[len - 1];\n        } else {\n            lps[i] = 0; i += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    lps\n}\n\nfn kmp(text: &amp;[u8], pattern: &amp;[u8]) -> Vec&lt;usize&gt; {\n    let n = text.len(); let m = pattern.len();\n    if m == 0 || m &gt; n { return vec![]; }\n    let lps = build_lps(pattern);\n    let mut matches = vec![];\n    let mut i = 0; let mut j = 0;\n    while i &lt; n {\n        if pattern[j] == text[i] { i += 1; j += 1; }\n        if j == m { matches.push(i - j); j = lps[j - 1]; }\n        else if i &lt; n &amp;&amp; pattern[j] != text[i] {\n            if j &gt; 0 { j = lps[j - 1]; } else { i += 1; }\n        }\n    }\n    matches\n}</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Implement Rabin-Karp with rolling hash</li><li>Solve the find-all-anagrams-in-a-string problem</li><li>Build a simple text search engine</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>LPS array avoids backtracking in text</li><li>KMP runs in linear time regardless of input</li><li>Prefix function has many other applications</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 16: Advanced Bit Manipulation",
        "html": "<h2>Bitwise Tricks & Techniques</h2><p>Bit manipulation provides elegant solutions for certain problems and is critical for embedded systems.</p><h3>Common Operations:</h3><pre><code>fn is_power_of_two(n: u32) -> bool {\n    n &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; (n &amp; (n - 1)) == 0\n}\n\nfn count_bits(mut n: u32) -> u32 {\n    let mut count = 0;\n    while n &gt; 0 { n &amp;= n - 1; count += 1; }\n    count\n}\n\nfn get_bit(num: u32, pos: u32) -> bool {\n    (num &gt;&gt; pos) &amp; 1 == 1\n}\n\nfn set_bit(num: u32, pos: u32) -> u32 {\n    num | (1 &lt;&lt; pos)\n}\n\nfn clear_bit(num: u32, pos: u32) -> u32 {\n    num &amp; !(1 &lt;&lt; pos)\n}\n\nfn swap(a: &amp;mut u32, b: &amp;mut u32) {\n    *a ^= *b; *b ^= *a; *a ^= *b;\n}</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Find the single non-repeating element in an array</li><li>Implement a bit vector for a prime sieve</li><li>Solve subset generation using bitmasks</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>XOR is its own inverse for swapping and finding duplicates</li><li>n &amp; (n-1) clears the lowest set bit</li><li>Bit operations are extremely fast (single CPU cycle)</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 17: Concurrency with Threads & Channels",
        "html": "<h2>Fearless Concurrency in Rust</h2><p>Rust's ownership model makes concurrent programming safe. The standard library provides threads, channels, and atomics.</p><h3>Channels:</h3><pre><code>use std::sync::mpsc;\nuse std::thread;\n\nlet (tx, rx) = mpsc::channel();\n\nfor id in 0..4 {\n    let tx = tx.clone();\n    thread::spawn(move || {\n        tx.send(format!(\"Thread {}: hello\", id)).unwrap();\n    });\n}\ndrop(tx);  // Close sender\n\nfor msg in rx {\n    println!(\"Got: {}\", msg);\n}</code></pre><h3>Shared State:</h3><pre><code>use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};\n\nlet counter = Arc::new(Mutex::new(0));\nlet mut handles = vec![];\n\nfor _ in 0..10 {\n    let counter = Arc::clone(&amp;counter);\n    handles.push(thread::spawn(move || {\n        *counter.lock().unwrap() += 1;\n    }));\n}\nfor h in handles { h.join().unwrap(); }\nprintln!(\"Result: {}\", *counter.lock().unwrap());</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Build a multi-threaded web scraper</li><li>Implement the producer-consumer pattern</li><li>Use atomics for lock-free counters</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>Send and Sync traits define thread safety</li><li>Channels enable message passing between threads</li><li>Mutex provides mutual exclusion for shared data</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 18: Rust Macros",
        "html": "<h2>Declarative and Procedural Macros</h2><p>Macros enable metaprogramming. macro_rules! pattern-matches on tokens. Procedural macros manipulate the AST.</p><h3>Declarative Macros:</h3><pre><code>macro_rules! my_vec {\n    ($($x:expr),* $(,)?) => {{\n        let mut v = Vec::new();\n        $(v.push($x);)*\n        v\n    }};\n}\n\nmacro_rules! hashmap {\n    ($($key:expr => $value:expr),* $(,)?) => {{\n        let mut map = std::collections::HashMap::new();\n        $(map.insert($key, $value);)*\n        map\n    }};\n}\n\nlet v = my_vec![1, 2, 3];\nlet map = hashmap![\"name\" => \"Alice\", \"age\" => \"30\"];</code></pre><h3>Debug Print Macro:</h3><pre><code>macro_rules! debug_print {\n    ($x:expr) => { println!(\"{} = {:?}\", stringify!($x), $x); };\n    ($($x:expr),+ $(,)?) => {\n        $( println!(\"{} = {:?}\", stringify!($x), $x); )+\n    };\n}\n\ndebug_print!(x, y, z);</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Create a builder pattern macro for structs</li><li>Implement a custom derive macro</li><li>Build an assertion macro with nice error messages</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>Macros operate at compile time</li><li>Repetition patterns ($( ... )*) handle variable arguments</li><li>Procedural macros enable compiler extensions</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 19: Unsafe Rust & FFI",
        "html": "<h2>Raw Pointers and C Interop</h2><p>Unsafe Rust allows operations the compiler can't guarantee, like dereferencing raw pointers and calling C code.</p><h3>Raw Pointers:</h3><pre><code>let mut x = 42;\nlet r1 = &amp;x as *const i32;\nlet r2 = &amp;mut x as *mut i32;\n\nunsafe {\n    println!(\"r1: {}\", *r1);\n    *r2 = 100;\n    println!(\"after: {}\", *r1);\n}</code></pre><h3>Calling C:</h3><pre><code>use std::ffi::CString;\n\nextern \"C\" {\n    fn strlen(s: *const u8) -> usize;\n    fn puts(s: *const u8) -> i32;\n}\n\nlet s = CString::new(\"Hello from Rust!\").unwrap();\nunsafe {\n    println!(\"Length: {}\", strlen(s.as_ptr()));\n    puts(s.as_ptr());\n}</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Write a Rust wrapper for a C library</li><li>Implement a custom memory allocator</li><li>Use FFI to call Python from Rust</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>Unsafe code enables low-level operations</li><li>FFI requires careful memory management</li><li>Unsafe blocks should be small and well-documented</li></ul>"
    },
    {
        "title": "Lesson 20: Rust Design Patterns",
        "html": "<h2>Common Patterns in Idiomatic Rust</h2><p>Rust's type system and ownership enable robust design patterns that prevent bugs at compile time.</p><h3>Builder Pattern:</h3><pre><code>#[derive(Default)]\nstruct QueryBuilder {\n    select: Option&lt;String&gt;,\n    from: Option&lt;String&gt;,\n    where_clause: Option&lt;String&gt;,\n    limit: Option&lt;usize&gt;,\n}\n\nimpl QueryBuilder {\n    fn new() -> Self { Self::default() }\n    fn select(mut self, cols: &amp;str) -> Self { self.select = Some(cols.to_string()); self }\n    fn from(mut self, table: &amp;str) -> Self { self.from = Some(table.to_string()); self }\n    fn where_clause(mut self, cond: &amp;str) -> Self { self.where_clause = Some(format!(\"WHERE {}\", cond)); self }\n    fn limit(mut self, n: usize) -> Self { self.limit = Some(n); self }\n\n    fn build(&amp;self) -> Result&lt;String, &amp;'static str&gt; {\n        let select = self.select.as_deref().ok_or(\"SELECT required\")?;\n        let from = self.from.as_deref().ok_or(\"FROM required\")?;\n        let mut q = format!(\"SELECT {} FROM {}\", select, from);\n        if let Some(ref w) = self.where_clause { q.push_str(&amp;format!(\" {}\", w)); }\n        if let Some(l) = self.limit { q.push_str(&amp;format!(\" LIMIT {}\", l)); }\n        Ok(q)\n    }\n}</code></pre><h3>Practice Exercises:</h3><ol><li>Implement the newtype pattern for type safety</li><li>Use RAII for resource management</li><li>Create a state machine using the type system</li></ol><h3>Key Takeaways:</h3><ul><li>Builder pattern creates complex objects step by step</li><li>Newtypes prevent logic errors at compile time</li><li>RAII guarantees resource cleanup</li></ul>"
    }
]

